# The `<style>` Element

The last notebook covered inline CSS, where we add CSS directly to an HTML element using the `style` attribute. However, inline CSS is not very maintainable, especially for larger projects. In this notebook, we'll cover another way to add CSS to a web page: **internal CSS**, where we add CSS to a `<style>` element in the `<head>` of the document.


```{html autorun}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      p {
        color: red;
        font-weight: bold;
        background-color: yellow;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p>Hello, world!</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

The code above includes a `<style>` element in the `<head>` of the document, with CSS rules inside of the `<style>` element. We have the `color` property set to `red`, the `font-weight` property set to `bold`, and the `background-color` property set to `yellow`. So this is a way to add CSS to a web page.


## Selectors

In the CSS example above, we have a `p` before the declarations. This is called a **selector**. A selector is a way to specify which elements the CSS should apply to. In this case, we're saying that this CSS should apply to all `<p>` elements. Then, we have curly braces to start and end the set of declarations that will apply to the `<p>` elements.

So if we added more `<p>` elements, they would all have red text, bold text, and a yellow background.

```{html autorun}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      p {
        color: red;
        font-weight: bold;
        background-color: yellow;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <p>Hello, world!</p>
    <p>This is another paragraph! The same style rules apply.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

### Multiple Selectors

We could also add a selector for `<h1>` elements, like this:

```{html autorun}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <style>
      p {
        color: red;
        font-weight: bold;
        background-color: yellow;
      }
      h1 {
        color: blue;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Heading</h1>
    <p>Hello, world!</p>
    <p>This is another paragraph!</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

Now we have two selectors: one for `<p>` elements and one for `<h1>` elements, and the `<h1>` element shows up blue. Selectors have their own syntax, which we will cover in more detail later.

## One Page at a Time

There is a limitation worth knowing before we go further. **A `<style>` element applies only to the page it is written in.** The rules above style this document, and nothing else.

That is fine for one page. A real site has many: a main page, an about page, an FAQ page, a contact page. If they should all look alike, every one of them needs its own copy of the same `<style>` element, and changing the color of every heading means editing every file, which is the same maintenance problem inline CSS had, one level up.

Notice, too, that the document now contains two languages. Everything outside the `<style>` element is HTML; everything inside it is CSS. The syntax changes at that boundary, and mixing up the two is a common early mistake. The next reading solves the one-page problem by moving the CSS out into a file of its own.

## Exercises

1. Add a `<style>` element to the `<head>` of the document below. Inside of it, write the following CSS to make all `<h1>` elements blue and all `<p>` elements gray:

    ```css
    h1 {
    color: blue;
    }
    p {
    background-color: gray;
    }
    ```

    ```{html}
    <html>
      <head>
          <!-- Create a style element below this comment -->
      </head>
      <body>
          <h1>Heading</h1>
          <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
      </body>
    </html>
    ```
    ```+solution
    <html>
      <head>
          <style>
              h1 {
                  color: blue;
              }
              p {
                  background-color: gray;
              }
          </style>
      </head>
      <body>
          <h1>Heading</h1>
          <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
      </body>
    </html>
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('style').should('exist').run('Failed to find a style element', 'Created a style element');
    assert('h1').should('have.css', 'color: blue').run('Failed to make the h1 blue', 'Made the h1 blue');
    assert('p').should('have.css', 'background-color: gray').run('Failed to give the paragraph a gray background', 'Styled the paragraph background');
    ```

2. Reading a selector

    ```{mcq}
    What does the selector `p` mean inside a `<style>` element?
    correct: Correct! The selector `p` targets every paragraph element.
    wrong: Think about which HTML elements match the tag name `p`.
    [x] It selects all `<p>` elements.
    [ ] It selects only the first `<p>` element.
    [ ] It creates a new paragraph element.
    [ ] It selects all elements inside a paragraph.
    ```

3. Add a CSS rule that makes all paragraphs (`p` elements) red.

    ```html id=page1
    <p>First paragraph</p>
    <p>Second paragraph</p>
    ```

    ```{css}
    /* Write your CSS here, as if it is inside a <style> element */
    ```
    ```+solution
    p {
      color: red;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page1
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('p').should('exist').should('have.css', 'color: red').run('Failed to apply red text to the paragraphs', 'Applied red text to the paragraphs');
    ```

